# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
import re, io
f='wallstreet-briefing.html'
s=io.open(f,encoding='utf-8').read()
orig=len(s)

# 1. Demote previous run's New tags
s=s.replace('<span class="tag new">New &middot; 12:24 PM</span>','<span class="tag">Carried &middot; Aug 31, 12:24 PM</span>')

# 2. Fix Chart of the Day heading / symbol disagreement (widget is NYSE:EIX)
s=s.replace('<h2 class="sec">Chart of the Day &mdash; PG&amp;E (NYSE:PCG)</h2>',
            '<h2 class="sec">Chart of the Day &mdash; Edison International (NYSE:EIX)</h2>')

# 3. New summary strip
new_tldr='<div class="tldr"><b>The Tape</b> <span><b>The recap this page has now refused three times came back under a different question, and it brought sector figures that contradict its own headline.</b> Asked for the day&rsquo;s biggest movers rather than the day&rsquo;s close, the same wrap dated <b>August 31</b> returned <b>S&amp;P 500 7,711.76 &minus;0.3%</b> and <b>Nasdaq Composite 26,402.42 &minus;0.5%</b> &mdash; <b>both Friday&rsquo;s verified closes</b> &mdash; alongside <b>PayPal &minus;12.7%</b>, <b>Friday&rsquo;s</b> move, and a claim that <b>technology (XLK) rose 3.2%</b> in a session it simultaneously describes as <b>weak on AI stocks</b>. <b>Refused in full for a third time.</b> What the live session actually shows: the <b>S&amp;P 500 down about half a percent at midday</b>, <b>energy the only sector higher</b> on <b>crude futures +2.24%</b>, and <b>communication services (&minus;1.9%) and utilities (&minus;1.6%) the two biggest laggards</b> &mdash; the latter <b>almost entirely two stocks</b>, with <b>Edison International</b> and <b>PG&amp;E</b> falling on California&rsquo;s failed wildfire-liability bill while <b>Constellation Energy rose 6.52%</b> in the same sector.</span></div>'
s=re.sub(r'<div class="tldr">.*?</div>',lambda m:new_tldr,s,count=1,flags=re.S)

# 4. New Lead block
lead='''<div class="note" style="margin-bottom:14px"><span class="tag new">New &middot; 12:51 PM</span> <b>A refused source came back through a different door, and the sector line it carried is what finally condemns it from the inside.</b> Asked this run for <b>the day&rsquo;s biggest movers</b> rather than the day&rsquo;s close, the same wrap dated <b>August 31</b> returned the same finished figures for a session that <b>has not closed</b>: <b>S&amp;P 500 7,711.76, down 0.3%</b> and <b>Nasdaq Composite 26,402.42, slipping 0.5%</b>, with <b>PayPal &minus;12.7%</b> named as the index&rsquo;s major loser on the <b>Advent&ndash;Stripe consortium walking away</b>. <b>Both levels are Friday&rsquo;s verified closes, which this page carries verbatim</b>, and the PayPal move is <b>Friday&rsquo;s</b>, already logged here at <b>&minus;12.63%</b>. <b>Refused in full for a third time.</b><br><br>&#9888; <b>What is new is that the third refusal did not need the level test.</b> The same return states that <b>ten of eleven sectors ended negative</b>, with <b>consumer staples &minus;1.4%</b>, <b>health care &minus;1.1%</b> and <b>communication services &minus;1.1%</b> &mdash; and, in the same breath, that the <b>Information Technology Select Sector SPDR (XLK) rose 3.2%</b> while the Nasdaq slipped <b>on weak performance by AI stocks</b>. <b>A 3.2% gain in the technology sector ETF and a Nasdaq falling on weak AI stocks do not describe the same day.</b> <b>A new diagnostic for this page, and a cheaper one than arithmetic:</b> where an internally inconsistent set of <em>levels</em> was evidence of mis-shelving at 11:50, an internally inconsistent set of <em>sector directions</em> is evidence of it too, and it can be spotted without holding a prior close in hand.<br><br><b>The live session, from sources that date themselves.</b> A midday read fetched this run has the <b>S&amp;P 500 down 0.55%</b> and the <b>Nasdaq Composite down 0.41%</b>. &#9888; <b>That is a seventh rendering of today&rsquo;s S&amp;P move</b> &mdash; the family now reads <b>&minus;0.43% / &minus;0.45% / &minus;0.47% / &minus;0.5% / &minus;0.55%</b> across the session &mdash; and, as with the six before it, <b>none is adopted</b>. <b>The spread is now twelve basis points wide and drifting lower through the day</b>, which is what a market falling gradually looks like from five separate clocks; it is not a disagreement to be resolved by picking one. <b>No index level is published for the live session</b>; the <b>Weekly Scorecard below still holds official closes only</b>.<br><br><b>The sector split sharpened and its two halves have different causes.</b> Reporting fetched this run puts <b>communication services &minus;1.9%</b> and <b>utilities &minus;1.6%</b> as the two biggest laggards, with <b>energy the only S&amp;P 500 sector in positive territory</b> &mdash; the group <b>up about 2%</b> on the session and <b>more than 6% for the month</b>, on <b>crude oil futures +2.24%</b>. Newly named on the downside: <b>Alphabet &minus;2.49%</b>, <b>Amazon &minus;1.69%</b> and <b>Boeing &minus;1.34%</b>; on the upside, besides Chevron, <b>Walmart +0.77%</b> and <b>Cisco Systems +0.67%</b>. &#9888; <b>Chevron now has two renderings from two sources this run &mdash; +2.84% and +1.86% &mdash; and both are printed; Exxon Mobil is given at +1.70%.</b><br><br>&#9888; <b>The utilities line is two stocks, and the sector&rsquo;s own leaderboard proves it.</b> A utilities screen fetched this run has <b>Constellation Energy +6.52%</b>, <b>Ormat Technologies +4.64%</b> and <b>UGI +2.90%</b> leading the group on the same day the sector index is down 1.6%. <b>The sector is not selling off; two of its constituents are being repriced by a single legislative outcome</b>, and this page states that rather than letting a sector percentage imply a broad move it did not have.<br><br>&#9888; <b>Two rate and commodity figures were fetched and neither is promoted.</b> A Treasury note gives the <b>10-year gaining more than four basis points to 4.705%</b> &mdash; but <b>the article carrying it is dated August 10</b>, three weeks back, so <b>it is refused on date and not printed as today&rsquo;s yield</b>; the dated <b>&ldquo;around 4.7% on Monday after three consecutive sessions of gains&rdquo;</b> already on this page stands, and <b>the table still carries Friday&rsquo;s stated 4.73% close</b>. Separately, a return states <b>U.S. crude futures hitting $80 a barrel</b> on Monday, which <b>cannot be reconciled with the &ldquo;near $86&rdquo; WTI figure fetched for the same session two runs ago</b>. <b>Both are recorded, neither is published as the level</b>, and the commodities table is untouched.</div>'''
anchor='<h2 class="sec">The Lead</h2>'
s=s.replace(anchor,anchor+lead,1)

# 5. New movers cards -> insert at top of Movers & Drivers section
mv='''<div class="card"><span class="tag new">New &middot; 12:51 PM</span><h3>Utilities &mdash; the sector line is two names</h3><p><b>Edison International (EIX)</b> and <b>PG&amp;E (PCG)</b> are quoted this run at <b>&minus;21.0%</b> and <b>&minus;20.0%</b> respectively, after <b>California lawmakers stopped Governor Gavin Newsom&rsquo;s plan to insulate utility providers from insurer wildfire lawsuits</b>. A second return has <b>Edison cratering more than 22%</b> &mdash; described as its <b>largest one-day decline since 2001</b> &mdash; and <b>PG&amp;E down around 19% in midday trading</b>, <b>on track for its worst day since 2020</b>. &#9888; <b>Edison now has three renderings (&minus;22.3% / &ldquo;more than 22%&rdquo; / &minus;21.0%) and PG&amp;E four (&minus;16.7% / &ldquo;around 19%&rdquo; / &minus;20.0%); all are printed and none adopted.</b> The two record characterisations are each single-source and attributed. Meanwhile <b>Constellation Energy +6.52%</b>, <b>Ormat Technologies +4.64%</b> and <b>UGI +2.90%</b> top the same sector.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag new">New &middot; 12:51 PM</span><h3>Megacaps lead the decline &mdash; Alphabet, Amazon, Boeing</h3><p>Named as the session&rsquo;s largest drags in reporting fetched this run: <b>Alphabet &minus;2.49%</b>, <b>Amazon &minus;1.69%</b> and <b>Boeing &minus;1.34%</b>. On the other side, <b>Chevron</b> is the largest named advance at <b>+2.84%</b> from one source and <b>+1.86%</b> from another, with <b>Exxon Mobil +1.70%</b>, <b>Walmart +0.77%</b> and <b>Cisco Systems +0.67%</b>. &#9888; <b>No cause is attached to the Alphabet, Amazon or Boeing moves</b> &mdash; nothing fetched this run offers one, and the energy names are explained by crude alone.</p></div>'''
a2='<h2 class="sec">Movers &amp; Drivers</h2>'
i=s.find(a2)
if i<0: raise SystemExit('movers anchor missing')
j=s.find('<div class="cards"',i)
k=s.find('>',j)+1
s=s[:k]+mv+s[k:]

# 6. Sources footer append
src='<p><b>Sources checked this run &mdash; 12:51 PM:</b> <a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/stock-market-news-aug-31-082900315.html">Zacks/Yahoo &ldquo;Stock Market News for Aug 31, 2026&rdquo; (refused &mdash; finished figures for an open session, third refusal)</a> &middot; <a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/article/energy-stocks-lead-in-subdued-final-trading-day-of-august-utilities-under-pressure-alphacheck-142014111.html">Yahoo Finance AlphaCheck &mdash; energy leads, utilities under pressure</a> &middot; <a href="https://seekingalpha.com/news/4638186-ten-top-performing-utilities-stocks-as-august-comes-to-a-close">Seeking Alpha &mdash; top-performing utilities as August closes</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-08-31/s-p-500-falls-amid-oil-spike-as-middle-east-conflict-intensifies">Bloomberg &mdash; S&amp;P 500 falls amid oil spike</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.thestreet.com/stock-market-today/stock-market-today-dow-jones-sp-500-nasdaq-updates-aug-31-2026">TheStreet &mdash; Aug 31 live market updates</a> &middot; <a href="https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/government-bond-yield">Trading Economics &mdash; US 10-year yield</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/10/us-treasury-yields-investors-eye-key-inflation-data-.html">CNBC 10-year note (dated Aug 10 &mdash; refused as today&rsquo;s yield)</a></p>'
m=re.search(r'<h2 class="sec"[^>]*>Sources</h2>',s)
if not m:
    m=re.search(r'<footer[^>]*>',s)
    if not m: raise SystemExit('no sources footer')
    s=s[:m.end()]+src+s[m.end():]
else:
    s=s[:m.end()]+src+s[m.end():]

io.open(f,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)
print('ws ok',orig,'->',len(s))
